clueweb09-en0005-33-25665.180 Animal Clinical Investigation: Publications cancer. [HTML] [PDF] Publications by ACI Faculty and Staff The ACI faculty and network members are the top professionals in the field. Their ongoing research in animal medicine is often published in leading journals and presented at national and international meetings. You may view their research findings in PDF or HTML format. [] PetMedicus Laboratories Receives MUMS Designation for Ondansetron (VML-028) in Cisplatin-Induced Emesis PetMedicus Laboratories, a client of ACI, was officially granted Minor Use and Minor Species (MUMS) status for their generic version of ondansetron (VML-028) for use in prevention of cisplatin-induced emesis on August 24, 2007 (http://www.fda.gov/cvm/MumsDesigList.htm). ACI played an important role in helping PetMedicus obtain this designation, representing only the second approved application for minor use in a major species of a Center for Veterinary Medicine (CVM) drug candidate. The designation was, in part, due to the completion of epidemiologic surveys by the ACI oncology network sites. These surveys provided essential population data that enabled the CVM to determine that the PetMedicus application fulfilled the requirements for minor use of a potential drug candidate. Overview of MUMS The US Food and Drug Administration (FDA) created the Minor Use and Minor Species Animal Health Act of 2004 (MUMS
clueweb09-en0009-61-32953.0 Lymphoma clinical trials for cats and dogs. [] CALIFORNIA Study: Evaluation of Genistein Combined Polysaccharide (GCP) Location: University of California Veterinary Hospital Davis, CA Phone: (530) 752-1393, ext 431 www.vmth.ucdavis.edu/vmth/services/oncology/oncology.html Eligibility: Dogs with high grade lymphoma that have not been treated with chemotherapy or steroids whose owners decline standard chemotherapy. Dogs must have enlarged peripheral lymph nodes, mild or no systemic clinical signs, and specific bloodwork values. Summary: High grade lymphoma represents approximately 80% of blood cancers in the dog and is the most commonly diagnosed and treated veterinary cancer. Despite treatment with aggressive multi-drug chemotherapy protocols, the median survival of dogs with high grade lymphoma is less than one year. Without treatment, life expectancy is only 4-6 weeks. GCP is a product of soy extract with no known toxicity which has shown anti-cancer activity in the laboratory. The purpose of this study is to determine the efficacy and safety of GCP for the treatment of dog high grade lymphoma. The side effects of GCP in the dog is unknown, but soft stool or diarrhea has occurred in other species at high doses. Requirements and incentives: Clients must be able to give oral medications (in capsule form) to their dogs. Follow-up at UC Davis is required at
clueweb09-en0009-61-32953.180 Lymphoma clinical trials for cats and dogs. must be able to give oral medications (in capsule form) to their dogs. Follow-up at UC Davis is required at one week intervals for 4 weeks and then at 2 week intervals through 2 months after enrollment. Costs covered include hospital visits, bloodwork and biopsy charges, and the study drug. Dogs must stay at UC Davis for 24 hours after the first dose of GCP only for blood and serial lymph node biopsy collection. Costs not covered by the study include additional medications not specifically called for in the study and treatment or hospitalization for progressive lymphoma or side effects from treatment. Once the patient is enrolled, the study pays for the cost of the hospital visits (associated with the disease), bloodwork, biopsy charges, and the study drug. For more information regarding this clinical trial, please contact the Clinical Trials Coordinator at 530.752.0125. Study: Evaluation of Denamarin in preventing liver toxicity during CCNU chemotherapy treatment in dogs Location: University of California Veterinary Hospital Davis, CA Phone: (530) 752-1393, ext 431 www.vmth.ucdavis.edu/vmth/services/oncology/oncology.html Eligibility: To qualify for this study, the canine patient must have a confirmed diagnosis of Lymphoma, Mast Cell Tumor or Hystiocytic Sarcoma. Patients beginning CCNU as chemotherapy with normal
